# Models

Every feature has its own folder under `src/models/<feature>/`. Inside that folder, **each model lives in its own file** named `<model>.model.ts` (lowercase, kebab-case). Each model file exports a property enum **and** an interface keyed by that enum.

## Folder layout

```
src/models/
  <feature>/
    <model>.model.ts        // one model per file
    index.ts                // barrel re-export (optional)
```

Example:

```
src/models/
  person/
    person.model.ts
    address.model.ts
    index.ts
  order/
    order.model.ts
    order-item.model.ts
    index.ts
```

## Required content of each `<model>.model.ts`

Every model file MUST export two things:

1. `EnumXProperties` — a string enum listing every property of the model. Member name in `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`, value in `camelCase` matching the API/JSON field.
2. `IX` — an interface using `[EnumXProperties.X]` as keys (never raw string keys).

```ts
// src/models/person/person.model.ts
export enum EnumPersonProperties {
  NAME = "name",
  SEX = "sex",
  AGE = "age",
}

export interface IPerson {
  [EnumPersonProperties.NAME]: string;
  [EnumPersonProperties.SEX]: "male" | "female";
  [EnumPersonProperties.AGE]: number;
}
```

## Consuming a model — always go through the enum

When reading or writing properties on a value typed as `IX`, **always** access the field through `EnumXProperties.X`. Never hardcode the raw string key.

```ts
import { EnumPersonProperties, IPerson } from "@src/models/person/person.model";

function greet(person: IPerson) {
  // GOOD — property enum
  const name = person[EnumPersonProperties.NAME];
  return `Hello ${name}`;
}

function rename(person: IPerson, newName: string): IPerson {
  return {
    ...person,
    [EnumPersonProperties.NAME]: newName,
  };
}
```

```ts
// BAD — hardcoded string key
const name = person.name;
person.name = "Alice";
```

This also applies to:

- `react-hook-form` field names: `register(EnumPersonProperties.NAME)`, `setValue(EnumPersonProperties.NAME, ...)`.
- Redux store `getData` / `setData` payloads (see `redux-store` rule).
- Table column `dataIndex`, sort keys, filter keys.

## Naming conventions

| Thing | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Feature folder | `kebab-case` | `src/models/order-item/` |
| Model file | `<model>.model.ts` | `person.model.ts` |
| Property enum | `EnumXProperties` | `EnumPersonProperties` |
| Interface | `IX` (PascalCase, `I` prefix) | `IPerson`, `IOrderItem` |
| Enum member | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `FIRST_NAME` |
| Enum value | `camelCase`, matches API field | `"firstName"` |

## Barrel `index.ts` (optional)

If a feature has multiple models, add `src/models/<feature>/index.ts` and re-export everything:

```ts
// src/models/person/index.ts
export * from "./person.model";
export * from "./address.model";
```

Then import via the folder:

```ts
import { EnumPersonProperties, IPerson } from "@src/models/person";
```

## Rules

- One model = one file. Do NOT put `IPerson` and `IOrder` in the same file.
- File name must end with `.model.ts`.
- Both `EnumXProperties` and `IX` MUST be exported — `IX` must use the enum as keys.
- No raw string property access on values typed as `IX`. Always use `EnumXProperties.X`.
- Place feature models under `src/models/<feature>/`, not flat in `src/models/`.
